WebCore: the curl cookie jar syncs nothing and keeps its journal and lock.

A page's document.cookie read is a synchronous message to the NetworkProcess, whose main thread also runs
every cookie write. With the jar in rollback-journal mode and SyncNormal each write fsynced the database
and a journal it created and deleted, and on LazyOS an fsync flushes the whole volume: the page stopped for
hundreds of milliseconds at a time. See the comment in the patch.

--- a/Source/WebCore/platform/network/curl/CookieJarDB.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/platform/network/curl/CookieJarDB.cpp
@@ -168,7 +168,23 @@
         }
     }
 
+#if PLATFORM(LAZYOS)
+    // LazyOS: this SQLite is built without WAL, so the jar is in rollback-journal mode, and every cookie
+    // statement is a transaction of its own: a journal created and deleted, the database and the journal
+    // each fsynced. A page that sets and reads cookies all the time (ad and consent scripts) kept the
+    // NetworkProcess's main thread in fsync, and every document.cookie read, a synchronous message from
+    // the page's process, waited behind it: the page itself stopped for hundreds of milliseconds at a time.
+    // The jar is not worth that: nothing is synced (the kernel's block cache commits every few seconds, in
+    // an order that keeps the volume consistent), the journal is kept and truncated instead of created and
+    // deleted (a crash of this process still rolls a transaction back), and this process, the only one
+    // that opens the jar, keeps its lock (no hot-journal check before every statement). A power cut can
+    // lose the last cookies set, as other browsers' batched cookie stores do.
+    m_database.setSynchronous(SQLiteDatabase::SyncOff);
+    m_database.executeCommand("PRAGMA journal_mode=TRUNCATE"_s);
+    m_database.executeCommand("PRAGMA locking_mode=EXCLUSIVE"_s);
+#else
     m_database.setSynchronous(SQLiteDatabase::SyncNormal);
+#endif
 
     // create prepared statements
     createPrepareStatement(SET_COOKIE_SQL);
